  ("Where the pain comes from", "<ul><li><strong>Adductors:</strong> pain on the inner thigh next to the pubic bone, when squeezing the knees together.</li><li><strong>Pubic bone:</strong> pain in the central front area.</li><li><strong>Abdominal wall and inguinal area:</strong> higher pain that increases with coughing or sit-ups.</li><li><strong>Iliopsoas:</strong> deeper pain when lifting the leg.</li><li><strong>Hip:</strong> deep pain with twisting, which requires ruling out a joint problem.</li></ul>"),
  ("How it is diagnosed", "<p>Examination with strength and pain tests for each structure is the most important part. Ultrasound in the consultation examines the adductor tendons, the abdominal wall at rest and under strain, and the hip joint. MRI is requested when the pubic bone or the hip needs to be assessed in more detail.</p>"),
  ("Treatment and return to sport", "<ul><li><strong>Strength programme</strong> for the adductors, abdomen and hip, progressing in phases with objective criteria.</li><li><strong>Training load adjustment</strong>, without stopping completely when possible.</li><li><strong>Reconditioning</strong> with running, changes of direction and kicking before returning to competition, coordinated with physiotherapists and strength and conditioning coaches.</li><li><strong>Ultrasound-guided treatments</strong>, including autologous growth factors, in selected chronic adductor tendinopathies.</li><li><strong>Surgical assessment</strong> only in specific cases that do not respond.</li></ul>"),
